title the dead-letter box -- a finished lap recovered out of a stash, Pond orbit one lands, %321 booked
prompt Open as HUSH under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, council rota row N mod 5, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- pick up the running thread, and work Pond completing the ai-jail enclosure under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think ListAgents answered no reachable agents, so I read the process table instead, which is the reading that actually answers the %291 question. Dream runs as codex inside ai-jail with --chdir /home/keeper/grain-dream and binds only three subpaths of /home/keeper/grain, so its --hot roster that started two minutes before mine reads its OWN clone and touches no path of mine. A peer named before a file moved, and the naming took a `ps` rather than a tool call.
think The cold roster ran 24 minutes over a clean index while I read rather than edited. It answered guards_run=77 guards_green=75 guards_red=2 tree_moved=no tree_at_close=6fc4f3092aff. Both reds are standing and gated: `declared_ceiling` is %295, a SHA-pinned MIND file this pier cannot edit without breaking the adaptation receipt, and `equinox_e123_living_pin_guard` is construction/REDS.md standing past its 24,576 -- the seat the card already names, since reds_fold.sh refuses any row carrying the word OPEN and all four closed pin rows carry it inside their bodies. Neither is agent-closable here, so both are surfaced rather than routed around and the line stands.
think The lane survey found the crux before I chose it. pond/bin/enclosure-policy sat on disk, 12 MB, built at 00:25 that morning, with no source anywhere -- git ls-files 'pond/apps/enclosure*' read empty. pond/bin/ is gitignored, so a build artifact was the only trace the night left, and it was a trace only because nothing cleans an ignored directory. git log --all --diff-filter=AD --name-only -- 'pond/*enclosure*' named a stash commit, and git stash list read one entry: "hush-crash leavings 1787927987". A finished Hush lap -- 557 lines of Rye, an eight-leg scan, a witness, two fixtures, its own log -- had been sitting there fourteen hours.
think The mechanism, plainly: I recovered the six files with `git checkout refs/stash -- <path>`, which reads the TREE object and carried mode 100755 onto tools/fixtures/pond_enclosure_policy_scan.sh. `git show refs/stash:<path> >` would have landed the same bytes at 100644, because a blob carries no mode and only the tree knows it (.claude/rules/exec-bit.md, proven on metal 20260827). I deleted pond/bin/enclosure-policy first so the scan's `if ! test -x "$BIN"` leg had to rebuild from the recovered source rather than reuse the crashed lap's binary -- a witness that reads a stale artifact proves the artifact, not the source.
think Reading the launcher for how the stash got made turned the red from an accident into a design finding. tools/f/fleet_round_open.sh stashes what a dead lap left at every round-open, and its own header states the doctrine: "Stashes are the fleet's dead-letter box; a hand or the lap itself re-derives them." The doctrine is sound. What is missing is a reader: nothing tells the NEXT lap that mail is waiting, so `git status` prints nothing, `git diff --cached --stat` prints nothing, and a full cold roster runs GREEN over a tree missing six files including the witness written to prove them. That reading is not incomplete, it is confidently wrong.
think The council rota read N = git rev-list --count HEAD = 3614, so row 4 -- Earth and Mercury, the concrete, whose sense is smell. Its dual seat is the marked value, and that foundation's Pond clause is one sentence long: "the customs house applies policy per mark at receipt -- what may be placed, what may be run, what waits for a word." That is the module I spent the lap landing, written before I read the row that names it. The fixed seat, Two Rooms, governed the README edit: policy-as-value had stood in pond/README.md as a HORIZON line since 20260618, and it crosses into the checkable room by the one door that law allows, which is that it earned a witness.
obs The record needed a truth repair before it could be landed. pond/enclosure_policy.kyri claims to READ tools/ag/agent-jail.sh and say what it builds, and it named four state binds as .claude-state, .cursor-agent-state and .codex-state. Those moved into the one `loops/<body>/` room on 20260827 under read-scope.md, and agent-jail.sh has read $LOOPS/claude, $LOOPS/cursor and $LOOPS/codex since. Four lines repointed, a fifth added for the conditional dot-claude.json bind, and the header now names the room. Every verdict is unchanged -- all five are project-bind-deeper paths that place -- so this repairs the claim rather than the policy.
obs What did NOT come back out of the stash is the honest half of this lap. HEAD had moved twenty commits, and three of the eight living pages the crashed lap edited carried further repairs by other hands: lotus/README.md and .claude/rules/checkpoint.md were repaired differently and better upstream, .claude/rules/quin.md was already byte-identical, and .claude/rules/lindy-first-crux.md shows the two hands each fixing one of its two negations. I restored none of them. Restoring a stale copy of a page two hands have since improved is how a recovery becomes its own red.
obs ERRATUM against session-logs/date/20260828/20260828-002135_the-policy-that-names-its-refusal.kyri, which lands unedited in this same commit as testimony of the lap that did the work. Eight of its thirteen `file` lines land as written. Five do not: lotus/README.md, .claude/rules/checkpoint.md and .claude/rules/lindy-first-crux.md were superseded upstream; .claude/rules/quin.md arrived by another route; and construction/ITINERARY.md is edited differently here, because that log's byte arithmetic (24,562 entering, 24,574 leaving) describes a card twenty commits ago. Its roster loom line -- guards_run=74, red=4 -- was true when measured and reads 77 and 2 today. The log is a true record of that lap and a stale record of this tree, and it is corrected here rather than edited, which is what dated testimony gets.
obs The card paid for its own new lines. construction/ITINERARY.md entered at 24,566 against a bound of 24,576 -- ten bytes -- so the Standing habit this red earned had to be bought. Two compressions the card's own single-stranded law already asked for covered it: the Now block restated the 20260827 role swap that stands verbatim four lines up in "Seated, and still live", and its Still-open paragraph recited detail the ledger keeps. The card closes at 24,519. The structural question the crashed lap raised stands unanswered and is Keaton's: whether a one-page card can hold six always-on lanes.
obs The REDS pin question answered itself across the rebase, and the second reading is the one that counts. On my own base the pin stood at 28,568 against a 24,576 bound with `equinox_e123_living_pin_guard` red, and my row took it to 31,500; I booked anyway, since the ledger's first law is that a red recorded is a proof and declining to record one to protect a byte count trades the lesson for the meter. Upstream then folded rows %304 through %317 onto a shelf while I verified, so on the tree that actually ships the pin reads 20,384 and that guard is GREEN -- measured after the rebase rather than assumed from before it. One red rather than two, and the one left is Mind's gated `declared_ceiling`.
obs Accrete-only held with nothing to resist. Not one markdown that instructs or references ai-jail moved: tools/ag/agent-jail.sh, tools/e/enclosure.conf.example, docs/ENCLOSURE.md and context/specs/enclosure-editors.md were read and cited, never touched, and the ENCLOSURE selector still reads ai-jail. All four citations were verified by reading rather than by trust -- docs/ENCLOSURE.md line 24 carries the do-not-widen roster the module implements, and enclosure-editors.md line 342 carries the --map and --rw-map spelling its header claims.
obs A peer parked a line while I worked: xy grew refs/heads/pier/diverged-20260828-141423 during my second fetch. HEAD and xy/main both read 0bb2b605f7 at package time, so this round derives on the anointed order with no rebase owed.
obs The hot roster caught my own edit, which is the roster earning the place the order gives it. `seed_link` read GREEN cold and RED hot: the paragraph I restored to pond/README.md linked ../expanding-prompts/20260826-033051_pond-completes-the-enclosure.md, and tools/fixtures/seed_link_scan.sh builds its shipped set from `allow` lines alone -- so a room carried under `scrub`, which expanding-prompts is at template-manifest.bron line 245, reads as outside the seed. One link took the ratchet from 848 to 849 against a ceiling that only falls. The guard names its own repair in its header: a withheld room is NAMED IN PROSE rather than linked. The plan is named now, the reading is back at 848, and the link that would have been dead for every seed reader never ships.
obs I stopped that first hot run rather than letting it finish, since it was already bound to report guard_red over a tree I was about to change, and a spent twenty-five-minute run answers nothing. The interruption left `.tpr` temporaries from tool_path_repoint_scan.sh visible in one `git status` and absent from the next -- its own trap cleaned them -- so the kill cost time and no tissue; measured after, zero untracked files.
loom guard=seed_link cold=green hot=red ratchet_before=848 after_edit=849 ceiling=848 repaired=848 verdict=ok
obs The row renumbered before it shipped, which is the derived-spine law doing exactly its job. I booked %316 from the anointed spine at 14:31 and upstream published its own %316 at stamp 20260828.143915 while my verification roster ran -- fifteen commits landed in that window, and the fold carried %314 through %317 onto a shelf. A published number never moves and mine was unshared, so mine derives above the fetched head at %321. The rebase also took upstream's Now block whole and re-applied this round on top: the card entered at 24,571 of 24,576 and holds the standing habit, the nib and one prior-lap row at 24,570, paid for by the role-swap sentence that stands verbatim four lines up in Seated-and-still-live.
